A||||3||dioxan||Engine stalls down hills||||6||
Z||000000||dioxan||08-22-2000||12:44 PM||||Hi All,<P>I have an 84' 2.5L that has started to stall consistently during the descent after a long hill climb.  The temperature is ok, and never gets above 210°F.  I wondered if it was the vacuum when going down the hill in gear with no throttle, however it still cut out if I was in neutral and coasting.<BR>When I first got the car, the engine would cut out with less than 1/2 tank of gas going around downhill righthand corners (not left hand??) which I assumed to be the fuel pump.  However, this current problem occurs with a full tank of gas.<P>Any suggestions?  If it is vapour lock, how would I go about eliminating it?<P>Thanks.||||reg||6
Z||000001||theogre||08-22-2000||01:10 PM||||check all the ground wires bolted to the block and look for any wire that looks pinched or is real short. The engine will shift every so slightly sideways in it's mounts. It could be pulling/pinching something.<P>you may also end up dropping the fuel tank. It can also shift around and pinch wires or the fuel lines. Also sounds like the fuel pickup may have an issue as the tank volume drops. You can fix all that at the same time.<P>You might be able to narrow down a fuel tank movement issue by carefully pushing some wood wedges/blocks between the left side of the tank and the body. They need to be in tight enough to stay w/o crushing anything. I say the left side because the tank shifts left when you turn right. It's not a guaranteed test but it might help. It's worth a try. See if you can also block the tank from moving forward.<P>If that solves the problem you will have to drop the tank and see exactly what's going on. You don't want to leave bare wires around a fuel tank. (Read the "official" TWA flight 800 report)||||reg||1
Z||000002||dioxan||08-22-2000||01:27 PM||||Thanks theogre, I'll give it a go.<P>dio||||reg||14
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-22-2000||06:45 PM||||Don't drive down hills, just coast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. Seriously, I had a ****er of a deal with a 2m4 that would run for a minute and then shut down. It returned a code of lean 02. Well, it was the 2" rubber hose between the fuel pump and the steel pick up tube. The hose had a longitudinal crack in it and would carry flow, but not pressure. ||||reg||1
